    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1242">

                <pb facs="1470" n="1272" sameAs="frus:frus1945Berlinv02/pg1272"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 PW/7–2145: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 1242<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Acting
                        Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Babelsberg</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1945-07-21">July 21, 1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Victory</hi>–188. Top secret to Acting Secretary of
                    State from the Secretary of State.</p>
                <p>Referring to your No. 69 of July 19, 1945<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1242-f1"/> on the
                    subject of questions asked by the press in connection with unconditional
                    surrender for Japan:</p>
                <p>I would approve of your making reply to the questions you mention in the
                    following terms: “The policy of this Government on unconditional surrender by
                    Japan has been repeatedly stated. Elucidations of this policy were issued by the
                    President on May 8 and on June 1.”</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Byrnes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
